## 1. Introduction
In recent years, pharmaceutical products as one class of the emerging pollutants, have attracted widespread attention, among them, the antidepressant drugs of abuse and the low efficiency of wastewater treatment plant processing, making their concentration level gradually increases in the environment [1,2], and might pose a serious threat to human health and ecological balance [3–5]. Venlafaxine (VEN), a phenylethylamine derivative, is a bicyclic atypical antidepressant, belonging to selective serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors (SNRIs), being widely used in the treatment of depressive mental diseases [6]. Due to that VEN is neither easily absorbed and metabolized by animals and humanbeing nor completely removed by sewage treatment plants, its concentrations could be several hundreds of ng ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ in environmental water bodies [7–9]. Admittedly, remediation is definitely expected to improve the water quality contaminated by SNRIs. In fact, regarding with nonbiodegradability of several persistent SNRIs, the inexpensive, sustainable and feasible techniques become necessary to deal with long-term effects of these pollutants [10]. However, several methods may be hampered for the potential applicability due to their poor cost-effectiveness and environment effects. For instance, membrane filtration may be highly selective, but practically expensive; while chemically oxidative treatments need some expensive chemical reactors and might result in incomplete degradation to produce some hazardous intermediate products [11,12]. Meanwhile, advanced oxidation technology, including chemical catalytic oxidation, electrochemical oxidation, photocatalytic oxidation, etc., has been widely used and developed due to its mild reaction conditions, strong oxidation capacity, and cost-effectiveness [13,14]. Chemical catalytic oxidation owing to its simple and efficient outstanding advantage has won the favor of scientific researchers. Particularly, catalytically activated peroxymonosulfate (PMS) stands out in numerous oxidant tests, because it contains $_{0-0}$ bond that can accept electrons and be activated to produce sulfate radical with longer half-life and stronger redox capacity [15–18]. Therefore, the design of effective catalysts is the significant point of catalytic oxidation degradation of VEN.
Among many metal elements, cobalt has been proved to be the most effective in catalytic activation of PMS, followed by ruthenium, iron and nickel, etc. [19]. Of course, cobalt can be found in many forms, such as $C0^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , $\mathsf{C o_{4}N}$ , Co-MOFs (Metal-Organic Frameworks, MOFs), $\mathsf{M C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ $\mathbf{\dot{M}}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ , Mn, Fe, Zn, Ni, etc.) [20– 24], etc. Zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) are a subclass of MOFs. ZIFs are topologically isomorphic with zeolites. They are formed from tetrahedral metal ions (e.g., Zn and Co) connected by imidazolate linkers [25]. ZIF-67 with isostructural SOD zeolitic topology is formed from cobalt ions and 2-methylimidazole. ZIF67 has a porous structure, a large surface area, and a big amount of active sites, therefore, it is applied to several fields such as a catalyst, adsorbent and so on [26]. However, ZIF-67 has poor stability and low electrical conductivity. Graphite carbon nitride $(g{-}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4})$ has a unique electron-rich nitrogen [27], which can be selected as an ideal loading material to improve the stability and the electron conversion efficiency of ZIF-67. In fact, the activation ability of $\mathrm{ZIF-67/g{\mathrm{-}}C_{3}N_{4}}$ on PMS and the stability of the whole composite catalyst are limited. As common elements in nature, iron and nickel are far less toxic than cobalt, and have catalytic effect on PMS as well [28]. Hence, they can be used as candidate materials for modification of $\mathrm{ZIF-}67/g{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ . $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}67$ synthesized by Chen et al. [29] showed excellent performance in activating PMS to degrade tetrabromobisphenol A, and ZIF-67@MIL-100(Fe) prepared by Zhao et al. [30] using hydrothermal method has also displayed obvious synergies in catalytic hydrogen production. Nevertheless, there are some important issues such as metal ion dissolution, charge transfer efficiency of the composite catalysts, etc. which need to be further investigated.
Herein, a simple and green synthesis method was used to fabricate $\mathrm{ZIF-}67/g{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ , which was further modified by adding MOF-74 (Ni) and MIL-100(Fe), respectively, to obtain ZIF-67/MOF-74(Ni)/g$\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{ZIF-67/MIL}\mathrm{-}100(\mathrm{Fe})/g\mathrm{-}C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ composite catalysts. The results showed that the performance of the modified composite catalysts for the catalytic activation of PMS to degrade VEN was greatly improved, and the stability was also better than that of unmodified ones. The mechanism in catalytic activation of PMS by the two modified composite catalysts was deeply discussed. At the same time, the factors affecting the degradation kinetics (PMS concentration, initial pH and temperature) were also considered and the leaching of metal ions was further investigated. In addition, the main degradation paths of VEN were inferred.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Chemicals
Venlafaxine hydrochloride (VEN, ${\mathsf{C}}_{17}{\mathsf{H}}_{27}{\mathsf{N O}}_{2}$ HCl, $299\%$ ), cobalt chloride hexahydrate $(\mathsf{C o C l}_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $299\%$ ), ferrous chloride tetrahydrate $(\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $299\%$ ), nickel chloride hexahydrate $(\mathsf{N i C l}_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $299\%$ ), 2-methylimidazole (2-MeIm, ${C_{4}}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , $298\%$ , 1,3,5- benzenetricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\cdotH_{3}B T C}$ , ${\sf C}_{9}{\sf H}_{6}0_{6}$ , $299\%$ ), melamine $\mathrm{\cdot}C_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{N}_{6}$ , $299\%$ , methanol (MeOH, $\mathrm{CH}_{3}0$ , $299\%$ and potassium peroxymonosulfate were purchased from Aladdin Chemistry Co., China. Sodium hydroxide $(\mathsf{N a O H})$ , sulfuric acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}S0_{4})$ , tert-butyl alcohol (TBA, ${\mathsf{C}}_{4}{\mathsf{H}}_{10}0^{\cdot}$ ), and absolute ethanol $(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}0\mathrm{H})$ were provided by Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., China. All chemicals were of analytical grade and used as received directly in this study. All solutions were prepared with Milli- $\cdot{\sf Q}$ gradient water with a resistivity of $18.2~\mathrm{M}\Omega~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ .
## 2.2. Preparation of $g_{-}C_{3}N_{4},$ ZIF-67, MOF-74(Ni) and MIL-100(Fe)
The preparation of $g{-}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}$ powder is mainly followed a calcination melamine method [31]. In a specific procedure, $_{15\mathrm{g}}$ melamine was added to an alumina crucible with a cover, and then placed in a muffle furnace to heat at $550^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ at a heating rate of $5~^{\circ}C/$ min.
ZIF-67 powder was mainly obtained by solvothermal method at room temperature [32]. The solution of $\mathsf{C o C l}_{2}$ $0.45~\mathrm{g}\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ in $5\mathrm{mL}$ methanol) was slowly added into the 2-MeIm solution $(5.50\mathrm{g}$ 2-MeIm in $18~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol). The mixture was vigorously stirred for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ , was left standing at room temperature for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , then collected by centrifugation.
MOF-74(Ni) powder was prepared by solvothermal method at room temperature. Firstly, solution A was obtained by dissolving $0.84\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ and $0.48\mathrm{~g~NaOH}$ in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ solution. Secondly, solution B was prepared by dissolving $1.31\mathrm{\g\NiCl_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ in the mixed solution of $25~\mathrm{mL}$ water and $50~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol. Thirdly, solution A was slowly poured into solution B, followed by dynamically stirring for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . The mixture was left standing at room temperature for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , collected by centrifugation, washed with deionized water and anhydrous ethanol for 3 times, respectively, and dried at 60 $^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
MIL-100(Fe) powder was prepared nearly same procedure as MOF-74(Ni) powder [33]. Simply, $0.84\mathrm{~g~H_{3}B T C}$ was dissolved in $11.4~\mathrm{mL}~0.9~\mathrm{mM}~\mathrm{NaOH}$ solution. Then, alkaline $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ solution was slowly poured into ferrous chloride solution $\cdot1.13\mathrm{~g~}$ of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}$ - - $\boldsymbol{4}\mathrm{H}_{2}\boldsymbol{0}$ in $48.6\mathrm{mL}$ water), and stirred at room temperature for $24\mathrm{h}$ .
## 2.3. Synthesis of ZIF-67/g- $C_{3}N_{4},$ , ZIF-67/MOF-74(Ni)/g- $\cdot C_{3}N_{4},$ and ZIF67/MIL-100(Fe)/g-C3N4
More information about the synthesis of MOFs and catalysts’ preparation could be referred in supporting information (SI).
## 2.4. Characterization
The phase structure was identified by an XRD-6000 X-ray powder diffractometer (Shimadzu). Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra were detected using KBr pellets on a NicoletiN10 spectrometer. Raman spectra were recorded on LabRAM HR Evolution spectrometer with laser excitation of $532\ \mathrm{nm}$ . A ZEISS Gemini 300 scanning electron microscope (SEM) with an Energy Dispersive Spectrometer (EDS) attachment was utilized to record the microscopic morphology of as-prepared samples. Meanwhile, the microstructure of the samples was observed using a FEI Tecnai G2 F30 transmission electron microscope (TEM). In addition, thermogravimetric analyses (TG) were performed in the range of $30-$ $800~^{\circ}C$ in a nitrogen stream at a heating rate of $10~\mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}$ on a TGA5500 thermal analyzer. The surface area and pore size distribution of the samples were calculated from $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms measured on a ASAP2460 surface area analyzer at $-196$ $^{\circ}C$ using the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) nitrogen adsorption method. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were performed with Thermo Fisher Nexsa. An electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectrometer (Bruker EMXPLUS) in continuous wave X-band mode was used to verify the dominant reactive oxygen species, $S O_{4}^{-}$ and OH, which were captured by the spin trapping agent 5, 5-dimethylpyrrolineoxide (DMPO). Finally, the dissolved metal ions in the process of catalytic degradation of pollutants were determined by Agilent 7700 ICP-OES.
## 2.5. Catalytic activity tests
In order to explore the catalytic activation of the prepared composites for PMS, the degradation tests of the antidepressant VEN were carried out. In addition, the degradation tests were conducted using PMS under the catalytic activation of various precursors and the physical mixure of some precursors(The results have been presented in the text and Supporting Information). VEN solution ( $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ , $10\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ) was placed in a $200~\mathrm{{mL}}$ glass container, followed by adding a certain amount of catalyst. The solution was stirred for $60\ \mathrm{min}$ at room temperature to achieve the adsorption–desorption equilibrium. Afterwards, a certain amount of PMS was added to initiate the degradation reaction, and $3~\mathrm{mL}$ of reaction samples were taken out at designed time intervals. The reaction was immediately terminated by adding $3~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol. The samples were centrifuged at $11,000\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $4~\mathrm{min}$ , and then filtered with a $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ PTFE filter for analysis late.
The concentration of VEN in the sample was determined by high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC, 1200, Agilent) using an Agilent HC-C18 column $150\times4.6\mathrm{mm},5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ ). The mobile phase was composed of $0.1\%$ formic acid aqueous solution and methanol at a volume ratio of 50:50. The injection volume was $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , the flow rate was $1\ \mathrm{mL}/\mathrm{min}$ and the detection wavelength was $225\mathrm{nm}$ . The products in the degradation process of VEN were qualitatively analyzed by high-performance liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry (HPLC-MS/MS). The chromatographic conditions were as follows: The mobile phase $0.1\%$ formic acid aqueous solution (A) and methanol (B), beginning from $95\%$ A and $5\%$ B, changing to $5\%\mathrm{~A~}$ and $95\%$ B within $5~\mathrm{{min}}$ , respectively, maintaining in this state for 5 min, and then returning to the initial state within 2 min. The mass spectrum conditions were as follows: Electrospray ionization source voltage was $4.5~\mathrm{kV}$ , nitrogen auxiliary pressure was $34.5~\mathrm{kPa}$ , scanning mode was full scanning with positive ion mode, and the qualitative scanning range of mass spectrometry was $m/z~50{-}330~\mathrm{u}$ . The calculation equations of degradation efficiency and degradation rate are as equation (1) and (2), respectively.
$$
{\sf R e m o v a l}(\%)=\left(1-\frac{{\sf C}_{t}}{{\sf C}_{0}}\right)\times100
$$
$$
\ln(\frac{C_{t}}{C_{0}})=-\mathbf{k}t
$$
Some of experiments for the influence factors of catalytic activation of the catalysts prepared on PMS to degrade VEN, including discussion, have been presented in SI.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization analysis
The XRD spectra of the samples for ZIF-67, MOF-74(Ni), MIL100(Fe), $g{-}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}$ , ZG, ZNG and ZMG are shown in Fig. 1. The XRD spectra of ZIF-67, MOF-74(Ni) and MIL-100(Fe) prepared by room temperature solvothermal method had highly similar XRD characteristic peaks with those reported in the papers [34–36], indicating that the prepared ZIF-67, MOF-74(Ni) and MIL-100(Fe) had high purity and well crystallinity. At the same time, a weak peak (1 0 0) at $12.9^{\circ}$ and a strong peak (002) at $27.5^{\circ}$ for $g{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}_{3}{\mathsf{N}}_{4}$ could be observed, and the latter corresponded to the typical interbeded accumulation and interlayer distance about $0.326\mathrm{nm}$ [37], demonstrating the successful preparation of $g{-}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}$ . In addition, the characteristic diffraction peaks of the composite catalysts had a good matching degree with the precursors $(g-C_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ and ZIF-67), suggesting that they successfully formed heterojunction among their internal components. More importantly, no new crystal phases were observed in the composite samples after pyrolysis, and the characteristic peaks of (011) of ZIF-67 and (002) of $g{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}_{3}{\mathsf{N}}_{4}$ were clearly observed at $2\theta=7.9^{\circ}$ and $2\theta=27.5^{\circ}$ , respectively. In addition, their peak signal intensity has a certain change pattern, in which from large to small, is ZG, ZNG and ZMG. Meanwhile, the corresponding characteristic peaks of ZNG and ZMG synthesized by the addition of MOF-74(Ni) and MIL-100(Fe), respectively, were not observed, possibly meaning that the crystalline structure of each component changed during the heat treatment, and the corresponding crystallinity was weakened.
Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) and Raman spectroscopy (Fig. 2) are mainly used to characterize important chemical functional groups in catalysts, especially for the characterization of MOFs. In Fig. 2(a), the samples of ZG, ZNG and ZMG share the similar infrare vibrational modes, basically, all of them have obvious absorption peaks at the bands 3671–3000, 1641– 1000 and $806~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which suggests that the basic structural framework is not changed by modification. In the FT-IR spectra of the composite catalysts, the broad absorption peaks in the range of $3671{-}3000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are attributed to the vibration of NAH and the $_{0-\mathrm{H}}$ bonds formed by of absorbing water for the $g{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}_{3}{\mathsf{N}}_{4}$ and other components in the catalysts [38,39]. The peaks for ZG at 1641– $1000\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are mainly attributed to the $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ and $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ bonds vibrations from the triazine ring $(g\mathrm{-}C_{3}\ensuremath{\mathsf{N}}_{4}$ and 2-MeIm), while the absorption bands for ZNG and ZMG around $1641-1000{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ correspond to the stretching vibrations at bond CAN, $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ , $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{=}\mathsf{N}$ , $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{C}$ , $0{\mathrm{-H}}.$ , etc. which agree with the complex constructure of the composites and their precursors [40]. The peak at $806~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is assigned to the bending vibration mode of the carbon nitrogen heterocyclic in the heptazine ring units of $g{-}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}$ [41,42]. However, compared with pure $g{-}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}$ , the typical stretching modes of ZG, ZNG and
ZMG at 1641, 1564, 1417, 1319 and $1243~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are significantly strengthened, which indicate that a little bit of ZIF-67, MOF-74 (Ni) and MIL-100(Fe) were successfully introduced into $g_{-}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}.$ . In the Raman spectra of the composite catalysts (Fig. 2(b)), the peaks at 474, 692 and $1229~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ represent the tri-s-triazine ring units and the peak at $1461~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ represents the D band while at $1613~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ represents the G band $(g{-}C_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ , ZG, ZNG and ZMG) [43,44]. The peaks at $466~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $676~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond well to the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}={\mathsf{N-C}}$ vibration, which belongs to the bengding vibration peaks of imidazole rings, as a result, strengthening the characteristic peaks of the samples ZG, ZNG and ZMG at 474 and $692~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ compared with pure $g_{-}(\mathsf{\Gamma}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ [45,46]. The two peaks at 1475 and $1586~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are attributed to the $-\mathsf{C O O}^{-}$ in benzocarboxyl group, which exists in all of MOF-74(Ni), MIL-100(Fe), ZNG and ZMG. Therefore, in the range from $1351~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to $1681~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , ZNG and ZMG forms a wide absorption peak while ZG shows two peaks [30,47], showing the composite catalysts have been successfully synthesized. Combined with the analysis results of FT-IR and Raman spectroscopy, the strengthes of the composite catalysts at the corresponding characteristic absorption peaks show a similar change pattern, that is, the characteristic absorption peak intensities of the modified composite catalysts (ZNG and ZMG) are weaker than that of the unmodified composite catalyst (ZG), which is consistent with the characterization results of XRD. In addition, the corresponding characteristic peaks (XRD, FT-IR and Raman spectra) of ZMG are weaker than those of ZNG, indicating that the interaction between MIL-100(Fe), ZIF-67 and $g{\mathrm{-}}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}$ induced by MIL-100 (Fe) modification is greater than that of MOF-74(Ni), ZIF-67 and $g\mathrm{.}$ - $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ induced by MOF-74(Ni) modification.
The surface morphology and microstructure of all catalysts are investigated by SEM and TEM. Fig. 3(a)–(c) showed the SEM images of pure ZIF-67, MOF-74(Ni) and MIL-100(Fe), respectively. It could be clearly observed that ZIF-67 had a regular polyhedral structure and smooth surface, with an uniform particle size of about $340\mathrm{nm}$ · which was similar to that reported by Liu et al [48]. MOF-74(Ni) was an obvious bulk structure with the smooth surface, but the size span was larger than ZIF-67, indicating the heterogeneity of particle size of the sample of MOF-74(Ni). As for MIL-100(Fe), it had a structure similar to coral clusters, with more pronounced edges and smooth surfaces, and was larger in size. In Fig. 3(d)–(f) SEMs showed the surface morphology of composite catalysts ZG, ZNG and ZMG, respectively. It could be directly seen that the Co-MOF with neat structure was loaded on the $g{-}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}$ surface of ZG catalyst, indicating that it is feasible to combine ZIF-67 with $g{\mathrm{-}}C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ by ball milling and low temperature pyrolysis. However, compared with pure ZIF-67, after ball-miling and pyrolysis treatment, ZIF-670s surface smoothness decreased, but its framework had essentially unchanged. For ZNG catalyst, many folds could be clearly observed on the surface of large particles of $g{\mathrm{-}}C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ , while the regular and smooth polyhedral ZIF-67 and bulk MOF-74(Ni) disappeared, and only part of the neat framework could be vaguely observed. It is worth noting that although the morphology of ZIF-67 had changed after heat treatment, the basic framework still existed to form Co-MOF-based. The folds formed on the surface of the polyhedron and the combination of irregular massive particles indicated that there was a close bond among Co-MOF-based, Ni-MOF-based and $g{-}C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ , which also illustrated that the addition of MOF-74(Ni) significantly changed the morphology and structure of ZG. In Fig. 3(f), a prominent point on the surface of ZMG was that a large number of structures similar to folds appeared on the cracks of the massive structure $g{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}_{3}{\mathsf{N}}_{4}$ , which was different from that of ZNG (its folds are mainly distributed on the horizontal surface). At the same time, regular polyhedra and coral cluster structures made a big change, which also indicated that the addition of MIL-100(Fe) had a significant effect on the morphology and structure of the catalyst. The energy dispersive X-ray spectra (EDS) and elemental mapping (Fig. S2, Fig. S3 and Fig. 3(g)–(l)) of ZG, ZNG and ZMG were further performed. The distribution of O and Co had no significant synchronization, so it could also be determined that the O in ZG derived from the possible water adsorbed by the material (Fig. S2(d) and Fig. S2(e)). The Fig. S2(i)–(k) results showed that the distribution patterns of Co, Ni and O elements were highly similar, and the Fig. 3(j)–(l) fully demonstrated that the distribution patterns of O, Co and Fe elements were highly correlative, especially Fe and O elements,which could confirm the formation of heterojunction among of them. To further compare the structural differences of the synthesized catalysts, TEM and HRTEM were performed, as shown in Fig. 4(a)–(f). In all TEM images Fig. 4(a), $4(\mathsfit{c})$ and 4(e), the catalysts showed typical layered and curved morphology of $g_{-}(\mathsf{\Gamma}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ [49]. In Fig. 4(a) and 4(b), the TEM of ZG showed that some surfaces of ZIF-67 were connected with $g{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}_{3}{\mathsf{N}}_{4}$ , and the HRTEM of ZG further confirmed that the two surfaces combined to form a heterojunction. On the TEM (Fig. 4(c)) and TEM-EDS mapping (Fig. S3(a)–(c)) of ZNG, it could be clearly observed that there was a relatively uniform distribution of nickel-bearing nanoparticles on its surface (the nanoparticles were in a state of incomplete decomposition of Ni-MOF, which was confirmed by the XRD, FT-IR and Raman spectra of ZNG). Furthermore, the neat edges of CoMOF framework could also be vaguely observed under partial encapsulation of $g{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}_{3}{\mathsf{N}}_{4}$ . Combining with its HRTEM (Fig. 4(d)), it was testified that Co-MOF-based, nickel-bearing nanoparticles and $g{-}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}$ form a tight heterojunction. In Fig. 4(e) and Fig. S3 (d)–(f), the TEM and TEM-EDS mapping of ZMG could clearly observe $g{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}_{3}{\mathsf{N}}_{4},$ and the corresponding iron-bearing nanoparticles and the outline structure with Co-MOF-based were distributed on it. Especially, careful observation of the TEM images of ZNG and ZMG has found that ZMG contained a certain pore structure while ZNG was not, which indicated that ZNG and ZMG were significantly different in the way of increasing specific surface area (SSA). The former increased its SSA by forming a large number of fold structures on its surface, while the latter was by forming fold structures and pore structures which was more beneficial to provide effective active sites for activating PMS. Combined with HRTEM (Fig. 4(f)), in which the dark black represented the ironbearing nanoparticles, the dark gray and light gray distribution represented the Co-MOF-based and $g{\mathrm{-}}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}.$ respectively, and it was further confirmed that the there were closely bound to form a heterojunction.
In order to evaluate the thermal stability of the composite catalysts, thermogravimetric analysis (TG) was performed in $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere. For ease of comparison, the same tests were also carried out for pure $g{\mathrm{-}}C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ , ZIF-67, MOF-74(Ni) and MIL-100(Fe) (Fig. 5). The weight of pure $g{-}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}$ was almost constant when the temperature was less than $492^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , but when the temperature exceeded this critical value, the weight dropped significantly, indicating that the structure of $g{-}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}$ began to collapse, which was closely related to the preparation temperature of $g{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}_{3}{\mathsf{N}}_{4}$ [50]. ZIF-670s weight change was manifested in two stages. In the first stage, when the temperature was lower than $409^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ the weight loss of ZIF-67 was about $6.28\%$ , mainly due to the loss of water and solvent adsorbed. In the second stage, in the temperature $409-656^{\circ}C,$ the weight was reduced by about $68.67\%$ , due to the decomposition of ligand 2-MeIm [51]. For MOF-74(Ni), it was also divided into two processes. The first process occurred at $103^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , and then the weight began to decrease and reached a platform (about $74.0\%$ ) that was mainly due to the reduction of adsorption water and solvent. Then, at $395^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and reached equilibrium at a new point, which was dominated by the decomposition of ligand $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ [52]. Similarly, the weight changed of MIL-100(Fe) was fell into two processes, but the weight loss of the first process was from 30 to $86~^{\circ}C$ and then reached a steady state, followed by the secondary started at $336^{\circ}\mathsf C$ The internal reasons for the weight change processes of MIL-100 (Fe) were the same as MOF-74(Ni) [35]. Thus, the stability of the pure compounds could be preliminarily compared with that of $g.$ - $\mathrm{C_{3}N_{4}}>\mathrm{ZIF{-}67}>\mathrm{MOF{-}74(N i)}>\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}100(\mathrm{Fe}).$ By comparing the thermal stability curves of ZG, ZNG and ZMG composite catalysts, the corresponding thermal stability was ZG $(481~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})~>~\mathsf{Z}$ MG (441 ${}^{\circ}C)>Z{\mathsf{N G}}$ $(336~^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ , suggesting that the addition of MOF-74(Ni) has a greater influence on the thermal stability of the prepared catalysts, followed by MIL-100(Fe). Afterwards, the weight of the final pyrolysis products of the three composite catalysts was compared and analyzed. That was $\mathrm{ZNG}\ >\ \mathrm{ZMG}\ >\ \mathrm{ZG}$ , indicating that the heterojunction composite catalysts were successfully synthesized. In addition, via the previous XRD, SEM and TEM images, the morphologies of the modified catalysts ZNG and ZMG were significantly changed, while combined with the thermal stability of pure precursors $(g-C_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ , ZIF-67, MOF-74(Ni) and MIL-100(Fe)), it could be seen that after heat treatment at $300^{\circ}\mathsf C$ their morphology and structure should not be changed. It seems that there are contradictory between the results of the morphologies and TG performances of the modified catalysts. However, in fact the morphologies of ZNG and ZMG did change, which could be attributed to the fact that MOFs with $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ as the ligand could interact with MOFs with 2-MeIm as the ligand under certain heating conditions, resulting in a decrease in the thermal stability (Fig. S4) [53].
Fig. 6(a) shows the ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms of three composite catalysts (ZG, ZNG and ZMG). The ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption properties of ZNG and ZMG were significantly better than that of the unmodified ZG, and both of them belonged to a complex adsorption isotherm of the III and the Ⅳ category [54]. Simultaneously, according to the classification model of International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC), the mesoporous hysteresis ring of the composite catalysts mainly belonged to H3 type, indicating that the pore structure of the catalysts was very irregular. It was further inferred that was a slit composed of sheet granular materials, which was consistent with the block layered structure of the main component $g{-}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}$ of the catalysts. The Barrett-JoynerHalenda (BJH) method was used to analyze the pore size distribution of the catalyst (Fig. 6(b)). The addition of Ni-MOF and Fe-MOF significantly increased the mesoporous content $(<34.33~\mathrm{nm})$ and the specific surface area ( $\mathrm{7NG~61.95~m^{2}/g}$ ZMG $57.24~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , but ZG $22.84\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\backslash$ .
The chemical states of the main elements in catalysts ZG, ZNG and ZMG were investigated in detail by XPS analysis. As shown in Fig. 7(a) XPS spectral survey scan, elements C, N, O and Co could be detected in all composite catalysts, while elements Ni and Fe could only be detected in ZNG and ZMG composite catalysts, respectively. Some elements’ binding energies of composite catalysts including C 1s, N 1s, O 1s, Co 2p, Ni 2p and Fe 2p, were also explored in Fig. 7(b–i) and Fig. S5. The C 1s spectrum of ZG (Fig. 7(c)) could be divided into two splitting peaks with values of 287.98 eV and $284.68\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which could be attributed to the $N\mathrm{.}$ $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ and $c{\mathrm{-}}c$ bonds connected by $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ on $g{-}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}$ and 2-MeIm, respectively [15,55]. However, the C 1s spectrum of ZNG and ZMG (Fig. 6(f) and (i)) could be divided into three peaks. In addition to the above two peaks, there was $285.88\mathrm{eV}$ due to the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond in the ligand $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ [30]. The N 1s peaks at 398.58, 400.18, 401.18 and $404.38\ \mathrm{eV}$ , were assigned to the $C-N=C$ , ${\sf N}{-}({\sf C})_{3}$ , NAH and charging effects or $\pi$ excitation in the heterocyclic ring, respectively (Fig. S6(a, b and d)) [55]. The O 1s main characteristic peaks of ZNG and ZMG composites located at 531.28 (corresponding to the Ni-O bond or Fe-O bond) and 532.68 eV (the ligand oxygen bond of $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ $\mathrm{^{\prime}H_{3}B T C-O_{\ell}^{\cdot}}$ , Fig. S6(c and e)), respectively [52]. The strength of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC-O}$ in ZMG was significantly greater than that of ZNG, indicating its high integrity and conforming to the results of TG analysis. In the Co 2p spectrum (Fig. 7(b), (e) and (h)), the two peaks at 780.58 and 783.48 eV represented Co $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ at 796.28 and $797.98\ \mathrm{eV}$ were related to Co $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , and were accompanied by two satellite peaks at 787.06 and $803.24\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively [45]. As the peaks of 783.48 and $797.98\ \mathrm{eV}$ had the spontaneity of direct interaction between cobalt species and $g{-}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}$ , which reduced the charge density around the metal material, thus making itself shift towards the direction of high band energy [56]. Therefore, it could be determined that the peaks of 783.48 and $797.98\mathrm{eV}$ represented ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ , and the peaks of 780.58 and $796.28\ \mathrm{eV}$ represented ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ [23,35,57]. In the fitted Ni 2p region (Fig. 7(d)), the two peaks at 855.58 and 856.98 eV exhibited Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ , while at 873.08 and 874.58 eV showed Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ [58]. Based on the satellite peaks at 861.88 and $879.68\ \mathrm{~eV}$ , which corresponded to Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively, it could be demonstrated that $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ was located at 856.98 and $874.58\ \mathrm{eV}$ , and $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ was located at 855.58 and $873.08\ \mathrm{eV}$ [35]. In the XPS pattern of Fe $\mathsf{2p}$ (Fig. $7(\mathbf{g})_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ , six peaks were observed, where the binding energies at 710.78 and $713.78\mathrm{eV}$ were ascribed to Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ , at 724.08 and 726.48 eV could be attributed to Fe $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , while at 719.38 and $733.78\mathrm{eV}$ illustrated for satellite peaks of Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Fe $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively [59]. It was further verified that the peaks at 713.78 and 726.48 eV assigned to $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ , and at 710.78 and $724.08\ \mathrm{eV}$ belonged to $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ [56,60]. The above results showed that the composite catalysts were successfully constructed.
## 3.2. Catalytic activity
In recent years, the concentration of antidepressants in the environment has gradually increased from ng ${\mathsf{L}}^{-1}$ to $\upmu\up g\mathrm{~L~}^{-1}$ , and even to $\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ . Therefore, venlafaxine was selected as a model pollutant to explore the advantages of the composite catalysts synthesized in this study for the performance of catalytic activation of PMS. Before the catalytic reaction started, the adsorption capacity of all the composite catalysts within 60 min was tested, and the adsorption removal rates for VEN of ZG, ZNG and ZMG were $2.66\%$ , $8.75\%$ and $5.43\%$ , respectively. The main reason for the difference in adsorption behavior resulted from their respective specific surface areas. Subsequently, the adsorption of the catalysts in the next $120\ \mathrm{min}$ was further investigated, and the results showed that the adsorption amount was negligible, indicating that the adsorption balance was basically reached in the first $60\mathrm{min}$ , as shown in the short-lines in Fig. 8. Moreover, as a control, a group of experiments with only PMS were carried out, and the results confirmed that if there was no catalyst, the oxidant PMS could only degrade little VEN. Meanwhile, the results also showed that the catalytic activity of $g{-}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}$ on PMS was very weak, leading to the removal rate of VEN to be only $6.31\%$ . The unmodified ZG showed a certain catalytic performance for the degradation of VEN, while the modified ZNG and ZMG showed a significant catalytic activation. ZG, ZNG and ZMG catalysts treated with PMS in $120\ \mathrm{min}$ could remove VEN up to $27.75\%$ , $91.38\%$ and $100\%$ , respectively, as shown in the solid-lines in Fig. 8. It should be noted that the activation effect of MIL-100(Fe) modified ZMG for PMS was also significantly better than that of MOF-74(Ni) modified ZNG, and the removal rate of the former reached $94.73\%$ within $60\ \mathrm{min}$ while that of the latter was only $82.54\%$ . In conclusion, compared with the interaction between ZIF-67 and $g{-}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}$ in unmodified ZG, ZNG modified by MOF-74(Ni) and ZMG by MIL-100(Fe), respectively, could significantly improve double MOFs synergistic effect (the physically mixing experiments of precursors could highlight the point (Fig. S8)) [52,61]. Compared with ZMG, the catalytic performance of ZNG was weaker, which might be because the fold structure on the surface of ZNG has a stronger masking effect on the active site of ${\equiv}0$ exposed on the surface and acting directly on PMS, which could reduce the efficiency of the composite catalyst for direct activation of PMS.
Ambient temperature is also important factor affecting PMS activation. Therefore, 25, 35 and $45^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ were selected as the temperature effect to explore, and the results are shown in Fig. 10. With the temperature changed from 25 to $45^{\circ}C$ VEN complete removal time was also significantly reduced from 120 to $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ , and the constant values $k$ of the degradation rate were remarkably increase from 0.055 to $0.072\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . Consequently, the $k$ values of ZMG/PMS system at different temperatures could be further used to calculate the activation energy required for activating PMS by the following Arrhenius equation (Eq. (3)):
$$
\mathsf{l n}k=-\frac{E_{a}}{R}\frac{1}{T}+\mathsf{l n}k_{0}
$$
In order to explore the degradation mechanism of VEN, free radical capture experiments were carried out, and the results are shown in Fig. 11(a). By selectively adding free radical trapping agents MeOH and TBA (previous studies have confirmed that cobalt-based catalysts mainly produces OH and $S0_{4}^{-}$ [64] in the activation process of PMS), the former mainly acts on OH and $S O_{4}^{-}$ , and the latter mainly acts on OH [65], observing their effect on the removal of VEN. It could be seen that with the addition of MeOH, the removal rate of VEN decreased from $100\%$ to $12.60\%$ within $120\ \mathrm{min}$ . However, the removal rate of the groups with the addition of TBA only decreased by about $20\%$ . Therefore, it could be determined that both OH and $S O_{4}^{-}$ free radicals were generated in the process of VEN degradation, at the same time, $S O_{4}^{-}$ played a leading role. This results were also been confirmed in the study of George P. et al [66]. In order to further confirm the experiment rusults, 5, 5-dimethyl-1-pyrrolidone-N-oxide (DMPO) was utilized as the spintrapping reagent for free radicals OH and $S O_{4}^{-}$ [67], which could be reacted and generated stable adducts that could be analyzed by electron spin resonance (EPR), as shown in Fig. 11(b). Significantly, the EPR signal peaks of DMPO- ${\cdot}S0_{4}$ and
DMPO-OH were obviously noticed in the ZMG/PMS system based on their unique hyperfine splitting constant for $5\ \mathrm{min}$ (red) or $20\ \mathrm{min}$ (black) [68,69], in which $S O_{4}^{-}$ and OH were produced, meanwhile, being well consistent with quentching experiments.
Based on the above free radical capture and EPR experimental results and XPS analysis, the mechanism of the activation of PMS by ZMG was proposed as follows (Fig. 12). When PMS was added to the solution containing catalyst, the $\equiv\mathsf{C o}^{2+}$ on the surface of catalyst would react with ${\mathrm{HSO}}_{5}^{-}$ to produce $S0_{4}^{-}$ directly, while the $\scriptstyle\equiv\mathbf{C}0^{2+}$ coated by $g{-}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}$ could only transfer its own electrons to the electron-rich surface of $g{-}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}$ and achieve the process indirectly (Eq. (4)), and regeneration could be achieved by $\equiv\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ and ${\mathsf{H S O}}_{5}^{-}$ reaction (Eq. (5)). In the same way, $\equiv\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ on the composite had also similar process with $\equiv\mathsf{C o}^{2+}$ (Eq. (6) and Eq. (7)) [70]. In addition, since the standard redox potential o $\mathsf{f}\equiv\mathsf{F e}^{3+}/\equiv$ $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ was 0.77 V [71] and that $\scriptstyle\mathbf{of}\equiv\mathbf{Co}^{3+}/\equiv\mathbf{Co}^{2+}$ was 1.92 V [72], this was conducive to the generation of $\scriptstyle\equiv\mathbf{C}0^{2+}$ (Eq. (8)) by the interaction between $\equiv\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\equiv\mathsf{C o}^{3+}$ , and it’s thermodynamically possible. Through this electron transfer could effectively improve the regeneration of $:\equiv\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ on the catalyst, further ensured its high efficiency and stability. Finally, the transformations of OH and $S0_{4}^{-}$ were realized through Eq. (12) and Eq. (13). Similarly, the activation of PMS by ZNG also had a similar mechanism (Eq. (4), (5) and (9)–(13)). However, the significant difference between ZNG and ZMG in the actual activation of PMS to degraded VEN, was also mainly attributed to the catalytic performance of the metal element itself on PMS and the Eq. (6)–(11) of the redox potential of $\equiv{\bf M}^{3+}/\equiv{\bf M}^{2+}$ $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Ni}$ , Fe, Co) induced interactions [28,70,71].
