## 3.3. Reusability and stability of ZMG
In the practical application process, the reusability, stability and catalytic performance of the catalyst are the focus of research. Fig. 13(a) shows that the removal rate of VEN by activation of PMS remains high $(91.78\%)$ after five cycles of catalyst ZMG use (after each experiment, the catalyst was only washed with ultrapure water and absolute ethanol and dried for catalytic reuse). Meanwhile, after the first experiment of ZG, ZNG and ZMG, the metal ions in the sample solution were measured, and the results were shown in Fig. S11(a) and Fig. S11(b), and Fig. 13(b). The leached concentration of Co was $0.54~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ (ZG), those of Co and Ni were 0.44 and $0.56\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ (ZNG), respectively; and those of Co and Fe were 0.34 and $0.44~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ (ZMG), respectively, which were lower than the national emission thresholds of (GB 25467–2010) for nickel ion and cobalt ion $(1.0\mathrm{\mg\L^{-1}})$ in China. In order to explore the structure change of ZMG after five cycle uses, the used ZMG was characterized by XRD, SEM and XPS, and the comparison results are shown in Fig. S12. XRD pattern of ZMG sample in Fig. S12(a) displays the characteristic diffraction peaks has almost no variation after five consecutive cycle reactions. Meanwhile, the SEM image in Fig. S11(b) can directly observe that there was little difference in the morphology changes of ZMG before and after use. Lastly, Fig. S12(c) and Fig. S12(d) reveal an XPS spectrum of the used ZMG recovered from the five cyclic reactions, in which $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ could be still observed, which further proves that it has the excellent stability. The above results show that ZMG has good reusability, stability and potential application. Moreover, Fig. 13(c) also explored the change relationship of the corresponding TOC in the solution with time with the gradual degradation of VEN. The TOC removal rate was rather fair when the VEN was completely or nearly completely removed from the solution, reaching $51.32\%$ . Anyway, it is necessary to further detect and analyze the main degradation products of VEN.
## 3.4. Degradation of VEN products and mechanism by activation of PMS
Through ${\mathsf{B}}3\mathrm{LYP}/6{\mathsf{-}}311{\mathsf{+}}\mathrm{G}^{\ast}$ level [72] in Gaussian 09, Mulliken atomic charges, highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO), lowest occupancy molecular orbital (LUMO), molecular electrostatic potential (ESP) and front electron density (FED) of VEN could be obtained (Fig. S13 and Table S3). Theoretically, the more negative of the Mulliken atomic charges and ESP, the larger of the HOMO’s isodensity surface and the higher the value of $\mathrm{FED_{HOMO}^{2}}+\mathrm{FED_{LUMO}^{2}}$ which meant that the atom would be more vulnerable to attack by free radicals. Meanwhile, combined with HPLC-MS/MS detection results (Fig. S14) and previous reports, the possible catalytic degradation paths of VEN were proposed.
Based on the above analysis, 15 major intermediates were deduced in Fig. 14, and most of them have been confirmed in previous studies [62,73–76]. The primary protonation and secondary protonation processes of VEN occurred first in solution, which was confirmed by the configuration of the pH of 9.27 in the aqueous solution of VEN. On this basis, the hydroxylation process happened on the benzene ring or methyl group position of Path 1, resulting in the intermediate products P1 and P2, whose molecular weight were increased. At Path 2 and 3, the protonated hydroxyl groups of $\mathsf{V E N}^{+}$ or $\mathsf{V E N}^{2+}$ dehydrated to form P3 and P7 isomers. Then, under the attack of free radicals, P3 broke off cyclohexane to produce P4, and further removed small groups to produce substances with smaller molecular weights P5 and P6. Meanwhile, the P7 was substituted and oxidized to produce P8, P9, P10 and P11. The Path 4 was protonated VEN to remove tertiary amine to produce P12, which was further epoxidized and superseded to form carboxyl compounds P13, P9, P10 and P11. The last major path, Path 5, began with a demethylation process to form P14, followed by replaced with primary amine and decomposed ring-opening produce P15. Finally, the simpler molecules of P6, P10 and P11 could eventually be mineralized into small inorganic compounds of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ and $\mathsf{N H}_{4}^{+}$ .
## 4. Conclusions
Based on single MOF composite catalyst ZG, the composite catalysts ZNG and ZMG with double MOFs by facile processing were fabricated, and a series of catalytic degradation tests were completed. Degradation experiments confirmed that ZMG was more efficient in catalytic activation of PMS, and the interaction of $\equiv\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}/{\equiv}{\bf C}o^{3+}$ was a prominent link to enhance its synergistic effect. The interaction ability and stability of each component in the composite catalysts (ZNG and ZMG) were optimized; and helpful to reduce metal ion dissolution. In addition, when $g{-}C_{3}\Lambda_{4}$ fixed MOFs, the interaction between them and the formation of heterojunctions should be worth exploring. Free radical scavenging experiments and EPR further confirmed the production of OH and $S0_{4}^{-}$ , and the latter played a leading role in the degradation process. According to the analysis of VEN intermediates, it was found that the main chemical reactions in the degradation process were hydroxylation, dehydration, demethylation and tertiary amine substitution. In this work, the main mechanism of the reasonable modified ZMG in the catalytic activation of PMS has been proposed, which could facilitate the design and application of heterojunction composite catalysts in the future.
